{"body":"Thanks for reading and sharing this feedback. After looking more closely at custom DID methods, I'm reluctant to go that route here.\n\nA Radicle-specific DID method (say `did:rad-artifact:`) that resolves to a Document carrying `verificationMethod` (the iroh endpoint key) and `service` (discovery) is essentially `radiroh:` reframed as a DID method, with the W3C DID resolution machinery on top. That's a bigger commitment than minting a URL scheme, for the same end result.\n\nI also think the requirements you named are already covered by the existing design, just in non-DID form:\n\n- **Stable identifier**: artifacts are addressed by CID, which is the stable, content-addressed name.\n- **Verification method**: a CID encodes its codec and hash function, so it self-describes how to verify the bytes it names. The verification method is implicit in the CID, no separate document needed.\n- **Discovery**: the COB carries `AddLocation` actions signed by the publisher's DID. The list of locations is the discovery mechanism, and resolving each one is the consumer's problem.\n\nI chose `URL` as the `location` type for flexibility. It covers `https://` mirrors today and leaves room for `magnet:` or other protocols, with the URL scheme itself doing the typing: `https://` declares HTTPS, `magnet:` declares BitTorrent, and the iroh case wants the same self-describing property.\n\nThat's also the case for `radiroh:` over a bare identity URI like `did:key:`. In a flat list of URL locations, the scheme is what tells a reader how to fetch. `did:key:` would be silent on transport and only readable by convention, whereas `radiroh:` extends the pattern that `AddLocation` already relies on. Each entry stays self-describing, and there's a clean place to grow optional hints later without touching the COB schema.\n\nResolution is a client concern in this design. Just as `https://example.com` doesn't name a DNS resolver, a `radiroh://<endpoint-id>` URL doesn't name an iroh discovery method; the client uses whatever it has configured (pkarr DNS, mainline DHT, static, custom). The URL carries identity, the scheme carries transport, and the choice of discovery stays out of the COB entirely.","replyTo":"4f04c9a3e1985aae93a8bfd316cf9dc6841ab811","type":"comment"}